<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-05">
  <title>Week 5: Lebesgue Measure (2D) </title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Additivity-of-Outer">
    <title>Additivity of Outer Measure on Borel Sets</title>
    
    <p>
        Recall that outer measure is not additive on all subsets of $\mathbf{R}$. This means that there exists disjoint subsets $A$ and $B$ of $\mathbf{R}$ such that $|A\cup B| \neq |A|+|B|$.
    </p>

    <p>
        Thus outer measure defined on all subsets of $\mathbf{R}$ is not actually a measure. However, we will show that outer measure is a bonafide measure when restricted to Borel subsets of $\mathbf{R}$.
    </p>

    <p>
        This restricted outer measure, which is called Lebesgue measure, is the generalization of the concept of the length of an interval that we have been seeking.
    </p>

    <p>
        We start our proof of the additivity of outer measure on Borel sets by showing that outer measure is additive when one of the sets is open.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-Addivitity-outer-measure">

        <title>Additivity of Outer Measure if One of the Sets is Open</title>
        
        

        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose $A$ and $G$ are disjoint subsets of $\mathbf{R}$ and $G$ is open. Then $|A \cup G| = |A| + |G|$.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <proof>
        <p>
            If $|G| = \infty$ then both sides are equal, so we may suppose that $|G| &lt; \infty$.
        </p>

        <p>
            By subadditivity of outer measure we know that $|A \cup G| \leq |A| + |G|$. Hence we just need to prove the other direction.
        </p>

        <p>
            First suppose that $G$ is the open interval $(a,b)$ with $a &lt; b$. We may also suppose that $a,b \notin A$ because removing two points from $A$ does not change its outer measure.
        </p>

        <p>
            Recall that we defined the outer measure of a set by taking the infimum over all open covers of the set. So let $I_1, I_2, \ldots$ be a sequence of open intervals whose union contains $A \cup G$.
        </p>

        <p>
            For each $N \in \mathbf{N}$ let $J_n = I_n \cap (\infty,a)$, $K_n = I_n \cap (a,b)$, and $L_n = I_n \cap (b,\infty)$. Hence $\ell(I_n) = \ell(J_n) + \ell(K_n) + \ell(J_n)$.
        </p>

        <p>
           Since $A$ and $G$ are disjoint, $K_1, K_2, \ldots$ is a sequence of open intervals that cover $G$ and $J_1, L_1, J_2, L_2, \ldots$ is a sequence of open intervals that cover $A$. Thus
           <md>
            <mrow> \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\ell(I_n) \amp = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \ell(J_n) + \ell (L_n) + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \ell (K_n) </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp \geq |A|+|G| </mrow>
           </md>
            
            Thus $|A\cup G| \geq |A| + |G|$ and hence $|A \cup G| = |A|+|G|$ in this special case.
        </p>

        <p>
            By Induction it follows that if  $G$ is a union of finitely many disjoint intervals that are all disjoint from $A$, then $|A \cup G| = |A| + |G|$.
        </p>

        <p>
            Finally suppose that $G$ is an arbitrary open set that is disjoint from $A$. Then $G = \cup_{n=1}^{\infty} I_n$ for some sequence of disjoint open intervals $I_1, I_2, \ldots$ each of which is disjoint from $A$.
        </p>

        <p>
            Hence for each $m \in \mathbf{N}$ we have
            <md>
                <mrow> |A \cup G| \amp \geq \left|A \cup \bigcup_{n=1}^m I_n\right| </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = |A| + \sum_{n=1}^m \ell(I_n)</mrow>
            </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
            Thus
            <md>
                <mrow> |A \cup G| \amp \geq |A| + \sum_{n=1}^m \ell(I_n) </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp \geq |A|+|G|</mrow>
            </md>

            Thus $|A \cup G| = |A| + |G|$ and the proof is complete.
            
        </p>





    </proof>

    <p>
        Next we show that outer measure is additive if one of the two sets is closed.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-addivitity-when-closed">
        <title>Additivity of Outer Measure if One of the Sets is Closed</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose $A$ and $F$ are disjoint subsets of $\mathbf{R}$ and $F$ is closed. Then 
                <md>
                    |A \cup F| \leq |A| + |F|.
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    

    <proof>
        <p>
            Suppose $I_1, I_2, \ldots$ is a sequence of open intervals whose union contains $A \cup F$ and let
            <md>
                G = \bigcup_{k=1}^{\infty} I_k
            </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
            Then $G$ is an open set and $A \cup F \subseteq G$. Hence $A \subseteq G \setminus F$, which is open because it is an open set minus a closed set.
        </p>

        <p>
           If we write $G$ as $G = F \cup G \setminus F$, then $G$ is a disjoint union in which one set is open. Hence the previous theorem applies and $|G| = |F| + |G \setminus F|$. 
        </p>



    </proof>
    


  </subsection>

  </section>